@@ -0,0 +1,174 @@
+# CSV import of contacts
+
+## Why
+
+Sales keeps leads in spreadsheets and currently has to add them to contactbook one
+by one. They want to load a whole CSV file at once and get a report of what
+happened: which contacts were created, which were updated, and which rows had
+problems, by row number, so they can fix them in the spreadsheet and re-import.
+
+The file has the same columns as our CSV export (`name,email,phone,tags`). Files
+written by our address-book export script, which has `first_name`/`last_name`
+instead of `name`, are accepted too.
+
+## Interface
+
+Other code calls exactly this:
+
+- `src/contactbook/importer.py`: `import_contacts(repo: InMemoryContactRepository, data: str | bytes) -> ImportReport`
+  where `data` is the file content: raw bytes as read from disk, or already-decoded text.
+- `ImportReport` (dataclass, same module): `created: list[str]`, `updated: list[str]` (normalised
+  emails), `errors: list[RowIssue]`, `warnings: list[RowIssue]`.
+- `RowIssue` (dataclass, same module): `row: int`, `column: str | None`, `message: str`.
+- `ImportFileError(ContactError)` in `src/contactbook/errors.py`, raised when the file as a whole
+  can't be imported.
+
+## Terms
+
+- **Record**: one CSV row as a spreadsheet shows it. A quoted cell may contain line
+  breaks, so one record can span several text lines.
+- **Row number**: the 1-based position of a record in the file. The header is row 1,
+  and every record counts, blank ones included.
+- **Blank row**: an empty line, or a record where every cell is empty or only
+  whitespace.
+- **Data row**: any record after the header that is not blank.
+- **Applied row**: a data row with no errors. Its contact is created or updated.
+- **Rejected row**: a data row with at least one error. It changes nothing.
+
+## Acceptance criteria
+
+### Decoding
+
+1. When `data` is `bytes`, it is decoded as UTF-8. A leading UTF-8 BOM is accepted
+   and is not part of the first header name.
+2. When `data` is `str` and starts with the BOM character (U+FEFF), the BOM is
+   ignored the same way.
+3. Bytes that are not valid UTF-8 (e.g. a Windows-1251 or Windows-1252 save) raise
+   `ImportFileError`. Its message tells the user to re-save the file as "CSV UTF-8".
+4. No other encoding is attempted or guessed.
+5. Files with `\n` and files with `\r\n` line endings import identically.
+6. Passing anything other than `str` or `bytes` raises `TypeError`, not a
+   `ContactError`. This follows the convention in `errors.py`.
+
+### File-level rejection
+
+7. All of the following raise `ImportFileError`. In every one of these cases the
+   repository is left exactly as it was: nothing is created or updated.
+   - The file is empty: it has no content, or only whitespace or blank lines.
+   - The file is not parseable as CSV, e.g. it has an unclosed quote.
+   - The header has no `email` column.
+   - The header has no `name` column and also has neither `first_name` nor
+     `last_name`.
+   - A recognised column (`name`, `email`, `phone`, `tags`, `first_name`,
+     `last_name`) appears more than once in the header after case and space
+     normalisation.
+   - The file has more than 1000 data rows. Blank rows don't count toward the
+     limit; rejected rows do. Exactly 1000 data rows is accepted.
+8. The `ImportFileError` message for a missing column names the missing column(s).
+9. `ImportFileError` is a subclass of `ContactError`.
+
+### Header and columns
+
+10. The first record is the header. Columns are matched by name, in any order. The
+    match ignores case and leading/trailing spaces, so `" Email "` matches `email`.
+11. `phone` and `tags` columns are optional. A missing `phone` column is treated as
+    an empty phone cell in every row, and a missing `tags` column as an empty tags
+    cell.
+12. Unknown columns are ignored silently, with neither an error nor a warning.
+13. A file with only a header and no data rows (blank rows allowed) returns an
+    `ImportReport` where all four lists are empty.
+14. Output of `export_csv` imported into an empty repository recreates the same
+    contacts (name, email, phone, tags), with every email in `created`.
+
+### Name from `first_name` / `last_name`
+
+15. When there is no `name` column, the name is `first_name` and `last_name` joined
+    with one space, then validated like any name. If only one of the two columns
+    exists, or one of the cells is empty, the name is the part that is present.
+    If both are empty, the row gets the "name is required" error.
+16. When a `name` column is present, `first_name` and `last_name` are ignored.
+17. Name problems are reported with `column == "name"` in both layouts.
+
+### Field rules (per data row)
+
+All values are validated and normalised by the same rules that `Contact.create`
+applies, so imported contacts look exactly like contacts added any other way.
+
+18. **email**: an empty or invalid email is an error with `column == "email"`.
+19. **name**: an empty or invalid name (e.g. longer than 100 characters) is an error
+    with `column == "name"`.
+20. **tags**: the cell is split on `;`. Each piece is normalised like any tag
+    (`" Key Account "` becomes `key-account`). Empty pieces are ignored, and
+    pieces that normalise to the same tag count once. If any piece is invalid,
+    the row gets an error with `column == "tags"`.
+21. **phone**: an empty or whitespace-only cell means "no phone given". An invalid
+    phone is **not** an error. The row is still applied, the phone is treated as
+    not given, and a warning with `column == "phone"` is added for that row.
+22. If a row has more cells than the header and any of the extra cells is non-blank,
+    that is one error with `column is None`. No other errors are reported for that
+    row. Extra cells that are all blank are ignored. *(Rationale: non-blank extra
+    cells usually mean an unquoted comma shifted the data. Spreadsheets often write
+    trailing empty cells, so those aren't a problem.)*
+23. A row with fewer cells than the header treats the missing cells as empty.
+24. A row gets one error per column with a problem. For example, a bad email and a
+    bad tag in the same row produce two errors, one with `column == "email"` and one
+    with `column == "tags"`.
+25. A rejected row gets no phone warning. Warnings are only reported for applied
+    rows.
+
+### Duplicates within the file
+
+26. Emails are compared after normalisation, so `Ann@X.com` and `ann@x.com` are the
+    same email.
+27. The first **applied** row with a given email wins. Every later data row with
+    that email is rejected with an error (`column == "email"`) whose message
+    mentions the row number of the winning row.
+28. A rejected row does not claim its email. If row 2 is rejected for a bad tag and
+    row 5 has the same email and is otherwise valid, row 5 is applied.
+
+### Applying rows
+
+29. Rows are processed in file order. A rejected row changes nothing in the
+    repository: it neither creates a contact nor partly updates an existing one.
+    Errors in one row don't prevent other rows from being applied.
+30. **New email** (not in the repository before the import): a contact is created
+    with the row's name, email, valid phone (if any) and tags. Its normalised email
+    is appended to `created`.
+31. **Existing email**: the stored contact is updated and its normalised email is
+    appended to `updated`. This happens even if nothing actually changed.
+    - **name**: replaced by the file's value.
+    - **phone**: replaced only when the cell has a valid phone. An empty or invalid
+      cell leaves the stored phone unchanged. An import never clears a phone.
+    - **tags**: the file's tags are added to the stored tags. An import never
+      removes a tag.
+32. Each applied row appears exactly once in `created` or `updated`, and no email
+    appears more than once across the two lists.
+
+### Report
+
+33. `created` and `updated` list normalised emails in file order.
+34. `errors` and `warnings` are ordered by row number. Each `RowIssue.row` is the
+    row number as defined in Terms. For files with multi-line quoted cells, this
+    counts records, not text lines.
+35. `RowIssue.column` is one of `"name"`, `"email"`, `"phone"`, `"tags"`, or `None`
+    for problems that concern the whole row.
+36. `RowIssue.message` is a short, human-readable reason that a salesperson can act
+    on.
+37. Blank rows produce no entry in any list, but they still count toward row numbers.
+
+## Non-goals
+
+- Encodings other than UTF-8 (with or without BOM), and encoding detection.
+- Delimiters other than comma, including `;`-delimited files from European-locale
+  Excel.
+- Excel `.xlsx` or other non-CSV formats.
+- Removing contacts that are absent from the file, removing tags, or clearing
+  phones through an import.
+- All-or-nothing import on row errors. Only file-level problems abort the whole
+  import.
+- Dry-run/preview mode, undo, or reporting unchanged rows separately from updated
+  ones.
+- Files larger than 1000 data rows, and streaming/chunked import.
+- CLI, HTTP endpoint or persistence adapters for import. Those live in other
+  packages and will call `import_contacts`.
+- Thread safety (the repository isn't thread-safe either).
